@model string
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Localization
@inject IHtmlLocalizer<Diten.Web.SharedResource> SharedLocalizer
@*
    UAS-001 — the 403 surface, drawn INSIDE the shell.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `NotAuthorized.cshtml` runs with `Layout = null`; it is the 401 page and it is right
    to be shell-less, because at 401 there is no user and therefore no menu to draw. At 403 the opposite is
    true: the user is signed in, the shell is theirs, and taking it away leaves them with a dead end instead of
    a way out. So this is a PARTIAL, rendered in the page body, with the shell around it.

    WHAT IT REPLACES. The page it stands in must draw NOTHING else — no heading, no KPI cards, no empty table,
    no "+ New" button. A half-drawn screen reads as "no data" or "broken", which is the defect this exists to
    end (UAS-001 §1, §6).

    HOW TO USE IT, from a view whose data needs a permission:

        @@inject Diten.Web.Services.IPermissionSnapshot Perms
        @@if (!Perms.Has("auth.roles.read"))
        {
            <partial name="_AccessDenied" model="@Localizer["RolesTitle"].Value" />
            return;
        }

    ⚠ This is a DISPLAY decision, not an authorization one. The single source of truth stays the backend's
    `[HasPermission]`; removing this check breaks the screen, not the boundary (UAS-001 §4).

    MODEL: the screen's own name, already localized by the caller — it is the caller's resource, not this
    partial's. It is interpolated into the title so the user knows WHICH door is closed.

    NO TECHNICAL TEXT. "403", "Forbidden", "Permission denied" are never shown here (UAS-001 §5), and the
    message says what to DO, not only what failed.
*@
<div class="card diten-access-denied" role="note">
    <i class="bx bx-lock-alt" aria-hidden="true"></i>
    <h5>@SharedLocalizer["AccessDeniedTitle", Model]</h5>
    <p>@SharedLocalizer["AccessDeniedMessage"]</p>
</div>
